Trie advantages: inserts/finds traverse a path matching the word, sharing prefixes reduces duplication; autocomplete across a prefix becomes a subtree traversal, yielding all completions efficiently. Hash maps store exact keys; prefix queries require scanning all keys or maintaining inverted indexes, which is costly. Trade-offs: Tries incur higher pointer/structural overhead per node and can be memory-heavy with large alphabets or sparse usage; compression (Patricia/radix) or fixed-size arrays can mitigate this. Deletion is non-trivial due to shared prefixes. Real-world nuance: store frequencies at nodes to surface top-k suggestions, or prune with a capped DFS for responsive UI.

Example extension (enumerate):

```python
def words_with_prefix(self, prefix: str):
    node = self.root
    for ch in prefix:
        node = node.children.get(ch)
        if not node:
            return []
    res = []
    def dfs(n, path):
        if n.is_word:
            res.append(prefix + path)
        for c, nxt in n.children.items():
            dfs(nxt, path + c)
    dfs(node, "")
    return res
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests mastery of DAG shortest paths and cross-checks understanding of BFS/DFS-derived ordering vs Dijkstra's heap-based approach.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (DFS or Kahn)
- DAG single-source shortest paths via DP
- Edge relaxation and cycle detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function dagSSSP(n, edges, s){
  const g=[...Array(n)].map(()=>[]);
  const indeg=new Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){g[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++;}
  const q=[]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[]; while(q.length){ const u=q.shift(); topo.push(u); for(const [v] of g[u]){ if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);} }
  const INF=1e15; const dist=new Array(n).fill(INF); dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){ if(dist[u]===INF) continue; for(const [v,w] of g[u]) dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);} 
  return dist;
}
```

